# Manuscript PDF page 105

                               THE BRAID



   A trumpet blew a long note and went flat. Somebody laughed at it.
   “A braid. Every strand its own, and strong because of the others.”
She smoothed her dress. “That is what you are, Juan Carlos. The
braid.”
   The drummers and the horn players had split into two huddles.
Each was taking its time deciding that the other was the problem.
   “The curse of this island is not that we hate one another,” said my
grandmother. “People always say that. They are wrong. The curse is
that we were taught to hear each other as late.”
   I looked at her.
   “In 1937,” she said, “on the border, Trujillo’s soldiers held up a sprig
of parsley and asked what it was. Perejil. A person who could roll the r
went home. A person who couldn’t did not. Thousands.” She didn’t
raise her voice. “Trujillo sent the machete. Abertura bought the land af‐
terward. They didn’t hold the knife. They held the deed.”
   The old Haitian woman, across the yard, had stopped glaring and
was looking at the ground.
   “You see? They do not need us to hate. They only need us to hear
wrong. Each one hears the other half a beat behind. Late. Not from here.
And after a while we play like that.” Abuela finally turned and looked
at me. “Escucha el patio.”
   Listen to the yard.
   I listened.
   The horns, the drums. The shouting. The chimney creaking. A
moped. And under all of it, so faint I’d have missed it a week ago, from
a speaker bolted to the front of the colmado across the dirt: a bright
bachata with a pressed place in it, every eight seconds. The pressed
place.
   Both sides of the band were leaning toward it. Not the same way, not
at the same time, and neither could hear what they were leaning to‐
ward. Each heard the other as off.
   I got up.




